#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81dd0b82-3bdc-58b9-aace-0499be563ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539692} {:answer/id #uuid "92cbbb20-640e-5d85-8433-6e6a415bd113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539723} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cac4dc-2679-5603-a932-b379a986241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539763} {:answer/id #uuid "fd559331-35f7-58ab-877a-14827b89cf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539909} {:answer/id #uuid "fe52eb10-47ee-5fd1-bfca-3c219b4d2d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92cbbb20-640e-5d85-8433-6e6a415bd113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539723} :answer-field/id #uuid "b909ad7e-6288-5330-b18b-d4332e6e69e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b8aa940-8e5f-5fd6-a1b0-b18405dff657" :question/math-academy-id "q-53011" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6ada3cc0fcd02f363da922e9537d3f832cd616886da84f63e65085bd271aa3e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the rectangle above?\n\nI. $∠BCD$ is not a right angle.\n\nII. $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are parallel.\n\nIII. $BC=CD.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. A rectangle is a parallelogram where all angles are right. Therefore, $∠BCD$ is a right angle.\n\n- Statement II is true. The opposite sides of a rectangle are parallel, so $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are parallel.\n\n- Statement III is false. Opposite sides of a rectangle are congruent, however, $BC$ and $CD$ are adjacent and we see that $BC\\ne CD.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533ff24d-0dd5-597d-81d4-ef34db14a4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539623} {:answer/id #uuid "57efc2a7-dd61-536c-bba1-56239c4d994b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539629} {:answer/id #uuid "95a90f98-8016-522b-90ea-a421361fd65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539728} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7493e8-0792-51c0-8f9c-1ab66d7e9c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539808} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bb7450-f341-5f68-89ad-4d37a81dadfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $R$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "533ff24d-0dd5-597d-81d4-ef34db14a4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539623} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa685bd-830c-5c31-bbf0-5ff6dfa7d380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33255947-d8b8-558c-b5b9-74099a601ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-53031" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe3724e266b68939b97f07fd7f428173ff0693b0d309d085187e44a45ba466ac.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a kite?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a kite is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a trapezoid. It doesn't have two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, therefore, it is not a kite.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a rectangle. Since it doesn't have two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, it is not a kite.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Therefore, it has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, so it is a kite.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a parallelogram. Any two adjacent sides are not congruent with each other, therefore, it is not a kite.\n\nTherefore, only shape $R$ is a kite."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a142cb-5a03-56b5-9608-5de7dc8a4e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539543} {:answer/id #uuid "5158fa1a-17a4-51ee-8896-bddf8a29d1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539622} {:answer/id #uuid "7adc4555-752a-5e57-bcb0-83e8ec2f53d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539684} {:answer/id #uuid "ccadcf93-d50b-58d0-9015-33e93c374069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539831} {:answer/id #uuid "d45dc88e-eeff-51ce-b206-d2acb0e76ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$, $R$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccadcf93-d50b-58d0-9015-33e93c374069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539831} :answer-field/id #uuid "e718d457-5460-5935-99e9-b9a4df1c718d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ffd60c0-4297-5e49-a178-11a94ccb47c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-53038" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b15000ac7f2de0770140ba289a8f5224433bfb6570d985ab184eacba016c97c4.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rectangle?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rectangle is a quadrilateral where all angles are right with two pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. It has no right angles, therefore, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, all its angles are right, so it is a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rectangle since all its angles are right.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite. Since there are no pairs of parallel sides, it is not a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, shapes $Q$ and $R$ are rectangles. The shapes $P$ and $S$ are not rectangles."}]}
